<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gudala</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gudala were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribe of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. Around 1040 CE their chieftain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-ibn-ibrahim">Yahya ibn Ibrahim</a>, returning from pilgrimage, brought the preacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallah-ibn-yasin">Abdallah ibn Yasin</a> to teach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, though the Gudala initially rejected him. Ibn Yasin then found support among the neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamtuna">Lamtuna</a>, and the movement grew into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a>.</p></div>
